  "step": [
    {
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1,
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "Finding interior water damage can be a real shock, but acting fast is your best bet for minimizing the impact. Your very first step is to stop the water source if it's safe to do so – whether it's turning off a leaky pipe or closing a storm-damaged window. Next, and this is super important, if the water is near electrical outlets or submerged cords, shut off the power to that area at your breaker box. Never wade into water if you suspect live electricity. Once the area is safe, remove any standing water. For small amounts, a wet/dry vac might work, but for anything substantial, you'll want professional help. Start drying out the space with fans and good ventilation to prevent mold growth, which can start surprisingly fast. Protect any undamaged items by moving them out of the wet area. Remember, quick action is key because moisture can hide behind walls and under floors, leading to bigger issues. If you're dealing with significant water, or if you're worried about hidden damage or mold, calling in a professional like HH Remodeling LLC right away can save you a lot of headaches and expense down the road."
    }
  ],
